WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
You're watching ColdFusionTV.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.040
Hi, welcome to another ColdFusion video.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:17.240
Remember Nokia? You know, that

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:20.000
company that used to dominate the mobile phone industry

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.380
in the late 90s and early 2000s?

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:25.840
The company that could do no wrong, until of course, it did.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.100
Well, after a swell of unconfirmed whispers and rumors,

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:33.300
the company from Finland is returning in 2017.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:36.960
As most of you probably know,

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.660
Nokia was losing massive market share to Android

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.600
and the iPhone in late 2000s,

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.960
but refused to get onto the smartphone game early.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:46.720
When they finally did get into the smartphone game,

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:50.600
they took a gamble and decided to use Microsoft's OS instead of Android.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.600
This, of course, meant that even though Nokia's build quality,

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.160
camera, and hardware was pristine,

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.100
the main complaint from the users was that there were no apps.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.180
This didn't bode well

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:04.030
for Nokia, and eventually Microsoft
bought the device and Services Division

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:08.380
of Nokia. After this, they ran it straight
into the ground.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:14.680
Part of this contract between Microsoft and Nokia was that no Nokia branded phones could be released

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:16.520
up to 30 months after the deal.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:19.220
Now that 30 month period has just passed and

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:24.380
Nokia can create their own devices again
and wear their brand name proudly

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:29.260
According to the website, Know Your
Mobile, there's going to be two models : a

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:33.220
mid-range phone and a flagship handset. The flagship phone is set to go

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:37.040
head-to-head with the Apple iPhone and
the Samsung Galaxy S8.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:41.220
For those interested in the technical details, the rumors are saying that the flagship

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:46.320
phone is going to have the as yet
unreleased Snapdragon 835 CPU with

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:50.100
6 gigabytes of RAM. Take that with a grain
of salt, of course. So the interesting

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:54.310
thing is, Nokia itself isn't going to be
making the phone, but they kind of are.

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:58.720
I'll explain what I mean in a second. So
Nokia no longer has the manufacturing

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:01.720
facilities to make the phone but they're
going to be collaborating with a

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:07.080
brand-new Finnish company called HMD Global as well as the Taiwanese manufacturer Foxconn.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:10.960
The reason I said that Nokia kind of is going to be making the phones again is because

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.880
HMD Global was actually founded by veteran Nokia executives.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:19.960
These ex-Nokia employees
founded the company in May 2016 after

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:22.400
purchasing part of Microsoft's mobile
division.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:26.820
In a press-release, HMD said that they were going to develop, market, and sell the

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:31.880
Nokia branded phones and accessories
while Foxconn is dealing with R&amp;D and manufacturing.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.620
Nokia is going on an
all-out marketing campaign blitz.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:39.960
This includes contracting the largest
independent advertising firm in the UK,

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:43.540
MOTHER. And I think this is probably the
best part of this story.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.700
More often than not companies screw up major opportunities like this because they

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.090
just don't get the marketing and
advertising right. Advertising campaigns

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:54.600
must be memorable and resonate with
potential customers, and has to get that

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:56.880
sales leverage point across clearly.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.360
With previous clients like Google, Microsoft,

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:06.040
Coca-Cola, IKEA, Calvin Klein, the MOTHER advertising agency seems a promising fit for Nokia.

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:10.020
So looking from a broader
perspective I think it's cool that a

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:14.940
bunch of ex-Nokia staff got together and
form their own company, HMD Global, to

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.940
take Nokia back from the grave and make
it rise again.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.160
We'll, of course, see what happens, but I'm wishing them all the best.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.000
So that's just a quick summary and

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:26.340
update about Nokia coming back, but if
you want to know exactly what happened

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:30.000
to Nokia in the first place or want
to know about their history I have a

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.320
three-part series on this channel about
that exact topic. It's called "The Rise

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.820
And Fall of Nokia." I'll leave it in the
description if you want to check it out.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:41.550
Anyway, thanks for watching guys, and if
there's not a video before the new year,

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:44.580
happy New Year and I hope it's a great
year for you guys.

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:46.540
Cheers, and have a good one.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:57.500
ColdFusion, it's new
thinking.

